# LAB 6 

## Kernel Tasks
The most of kernel task lives in mem/zero.c. Where we generate a per cpu kernel task, which uses the per cpu zero_pending list, in ordre to reduce locking while in the kernel task.

In kthread_create() is where the kernel tasks are created with its kernel stack. The pid's are assgined decreasingly from pid_max - 1, so the first kernel task would have PID 65535. This has been choesen to not influence user tasks PIDs.


## Core Pinning (Affinity)


## Core Hotplugging
##### MACRO = BONUS_CORE_HOTPLUGGING

Cores can be powered off and on manually with sys_core_disable(num) and sys_core_enable(num) or automatically by the load balances. A disabled core, moves in a sleep loop, before that it moves its running task and its local run queue to the global run queue, stops its LAPIC timer. To enable it, we send it an IPI (IRQ_WAKEUP), after which it restarts its timer and enters the scheduler again. The same IPI is used to make a running core enter the scheduler right away when it should power off, instead of waiting for its next timer tick.

The load balancer, disables a core, other than the boot CPU, once it has been idle for 10 ms. When a new task gets in the global run queue, a sleeping core is started. Also taking account of the affinity if that was set-up for the specific task, so a task pinned to a core is not disabled by the load balancer. A core disabled with sys_core_disable() stays off until sys_core_enable() is called.

The core hotplugging has been thus bulid to take task affinity into account.



------------------------------------------------
# LAB 5
## Scheduler
The scheduler always runs the task that has used the least CPU time so far, which we call its karma.
A task's karma only grows while it runs, so CPU-bound tasks keep overtaking each other and end up with the same amount of CPU time, while a task that yields or blocks early keeps its lower karma and gets the CPU back sooner.
The timer tick makes sure no task keeps the CPU for longer than one tick before the next pick.

Runnable tasks are kept in a red-black tree ordered by karma, so picking the next task is just taking the leftmost node, and tasks with equal karma take turns. With n runnable tasks, picking, inserting and removing a task all take O(log n), and charging the running task is O(1).

A task that was blocked for a long time would come back with far less karma than the others and monopolize the CPU until it caught up.
To prevent that, we remember the karma of the last task we picked, which only grows over time, and whenever a task enters the run queue with less karma than that, we raise its karma to that value.
The task then rejoins level with the others, so it still runs soon, but only gets its fair share instead of being paid back for the time it was away.

Fork bombing works the other way around, as a task could keep handing its work over to freshly forked children, and if every child started with little karma, the work would always jump ahead of the other tasks.
To stop that, a forked child starts with the karma of its parent plus the CPU time the parent has used in its current turn, so the work keeps being charged no matter how often it moves to a new task. We added tests for both cases.

## Bonus
## Exec + Zero-Page Deduplication
##### MACRO = BONUS_EXEC_ZERO_DEDUP

First, we have to find the executable in the image. That is done inside sys_exec() which uses find_symbol() to find its location; afterwards we call task_exec().

On the stack of the function we make a replacement task, which is used to set up the memory layout. If that does not succeed we still have the current user binary we can return to with an error. Once we were able to inititilise the vma's, we move those to the current task, after we removed the current vma's from the current task. We transition the task frame and pml4 pointer, then we clean up the "replacement" task at the end.

For Zero deduplication we point to one global zero page when we page fault on non present or non written pages. task_shared_file_fault() is used to point to the physical pages used by the kernel for the user binary

## Sleep and Time
##### MACRO = BONUS_SLEEP_TIME

All time keeping is based on the TSC, which we calibrate against the HPET at boot, while the RTC is only read once to know the Unix time at that moment. Since we never set the clock, CLOCK_REALTIME is simply CLOCK_MONOTONIC plus a fixed offset. clock_gettime(), gettimeofday() and time() all share this calculation, and with the vDSO enabled they run entirely in user space.

For sleeping, clock_nanosleep() is the only new syscall, and nanosleep(), usleep() and sleep() are small libc wrappers around it. An absolute deadline is turned into a relative one using the chosen clock, so a deadline that has already passed returns right away. There are no signals, so a sleep is never interrupted and rem is never written.

Sleeping tasks are kept in a second red-black tree, ordered by the TSC value at which they have to wake up.
Putting a task to sleep takes O(log n), and checking for due tasks only looks at the leftmost node, so waking k tasks takes O(k log n) and costs O(log n) when nothing is due.

We do not program a separate timer for them, because the scheduler already wakes every task whose deadline has passed each time it is entered, which happens at least on every timer tick.
If only sleeping tasks are left, the scheduler busy-waits until the first one is due instead of halting. A task is never runnable and sleeping at the same time, so it uses the same tree node for both trees, and a sleeping task that gets killed is removed from the sleep tree.

