# LAB 2 bonuses

The use after free and the out of bounds tests rely on how the pages are mapped, which cause a page fault on their access. Because interrupts are scheduled for lab 3, these tests triple fault for now. And thus no kernel panic can be issued as the test framework requires. For that we put another cprintf() after the line that should page fault, if that gets printed means that the test failed as it did not page fault.

## SANITY CHECKS
### DOUBLE FREE and/or INVALID FREE
##### MACRO = BONUS_DOUBLE_FREE || BONUS_PAGING_INVALID_FREE
For these sanity features we do a page lookup, and if that fails, that means we free an invalid page or an already freed one. For invalid free we also check misaligned addresses. 

## OUT_OF_BOUNDS
##### MACRO = BONUS_OUT_OF_BOUNDS
For this bonus we introduce the interface `
int page_insert_guarded(struct page_table *pml4, struct page_info *page, void *va, uint64_t flags);`, that keeps adjacent guard pages unmapped. By keeping the adjacent pages unmapped, a page fault is going to be triggered by each out of bounds read/write. It was chosen for a new interface in order to not interfere with mostly how the buddy allocator works.

The test tries to insert pages around allocated pages, and it fails because of the guard pages. At the end it tries an OOB write which it page faults on. As mentioned above the interrupts are not there yet, but this should count as a successful test.

#### Write protect
For this we set the WP bit on in the CR0 register, which raises a page fault on writes on read-only pages even from Ring 0. 
The test tries to write to a read-only page and it page faults while trying it.


## USE_AFTER_FREE
##### MACRO = BONUS_USE_AFTER_FREE
This is mostly just a test that checks that removed pages are actually unmapped and not anymore accessible. Thus in the end the test tries to write to the unaccessible and in response it triggers a page fault. (see above)

------------------------------------------------
# Description of LAB 1 bonuses:

## DOUBLE FREE
##### MACRO = BONUS_DOUBLE_FREE

This feature checks whether if something tries to free a free page again and it panics if that is the case. It is implemented in `page_free()` where it checks before hand if 
`pp->pp_free == 1`, if that is true, then it panics.


## USE_AFTER_FREE
##### MACRO = BONUS_USE_AFTER_FREE

This bonus poisons a page after it has been freed in `page_free()`. And before giving out a page out in `page_alloc()` we check whether it has been written to that page. That means that a to be given out page either contains 0's or the poison. If that is not the case then it panics. The use after free write is detected when that page is being allocated again.

## OUT_OF_BOUNDS
##### MACRO = BONUS_OUT_OF_BOUNDS

This feature detects out of bounds writes into free pages. When a new page is being allocated, it is being checked if it either full of 0's or with the canary. In the case that is not true, that means that an out of bounds write happened. The canary is written in `page_free()`. Why the check for 0's in the page? That is in the case of an out of bounds write into a fresh page that was never given out.
Because we only detect afterwards out of bounds writes afterwards, the implementation for this feature is basically the same as the one for use after free.

## INVALID FREE
#### Macro = BONUS_INVALID_FREE

This features detects invalid frees. It detects if the page is inside of the page array or not. It also checks where the address is paged_info-aligned, to protect against the corruption of the page data structure. And it also detects if a page that is marked as used because it is used in a higher order buddy allocation, but it is not its head. For this check we mark all the tail pages of a buddy block with `BUDDY_MAX_ORDER` and the block order is checked in `page_free()` before free'ing a specific block. It panics in all cases of invalid free.
